Mid term (1.x)
========

These changes are unlikely to break the CLI, config file format or corpus JSON.

- use background color in heat map (blue-cyan-grey-magenta-red-yellow)
  (create color buckets based on letter frequencies so that each bucket
   represents about the same number of keystrokes)
- use font color for n-grams (green-<neutral>-red, if target is set)
- implement normalizing and mixing of corpora
- with normalized corpora, try if using u32 instead of u64 is faster for
  counting key costs and n-grams
  may also have advantages of preserving accuracy in conversion to f64 scores
- try if f32 for travel and scores has any perf advantage
- implement filtering of layouts (in rank and stats subcommands) by score-based
  conditions

Longer term (2.x)
===========

More involved changes that can break the CLI, config file format or corpus JSON.
Also some nice-to-haves.

- Insert Shift before first in a sequence of capitalized letter or shifted
  symbols
  - Promoting bigrams 'lU' or ' U' to trigrams 'l_U' or ' _U'
  - Drop 3-grams that include such bigrams
- add support for thumb letters
  - need a representation of thumb keys
    (are they part of the same hand or counted as separate for letter and
     n-gram stats?)
- Support bigger layouts up to full ANSI/ISO 60% (minus Ctrl,Alt,Win,Menu)
- [maybe] Change constraint configuration with one group for each constraint
- Constraint for soft-pinning keys. Maybe this could replace the ref_layout
  constraint. Could specify different weights for same-finger, same-hand,
  other-hand. And a global threshold.
- CLI changes:
  - update to newer version of clap
  - kuehlmak config (show config: weights instead of stats, update config file)
  - kuehlmak eval -i (interactive mode) (later)
  - [maybe] kuehlmak reset (delete kbl files)
- output options:
  - [maybe] visualize column-stagger (two rows on top)
  - output HTML or SVG (with color)
- add histogram plotting for population stats

Goals for 2.0
=============

The main goal is to count Shift in key, bigram and 3-gram counts. This also
requires representing more keys, including thumb keys. While I'm there, I
also want to support complete 60% layouts with numbers and symbols.

- 60% ANSI: full row-staggered ANSI layout with number row and all symbols
  55 keys without Ctl, Win, Alt, Menu, Caps, Tab and with a split space key
- 60% ISO: full row-staggered ISO layout with number row and all symbols
  56 keys without Ctl, Win, Alt, Menu, Caps, Tab and with a split space key
- 12x4 Ortho/ColStag + 2x3 thumb keys: 54 keys
- 12x3 Ortho/ColStag + 2x3 thumb keys: 42 keys
- 10x3 Ortho/ColStag + 2x3 tnumb keys: 36 keys (closest to v1.0)
- Bigger Hex layouts (Bumblebee 60% or Hornet 50%)

Allow letters and symbols on Thumb keys.

I need a way to specify the location of Space and Shift keys, optionally
one Space/Shift per hand.

Allow home-row mods (Shift on the same key as a letter/symbol).

Allow marking keys as unused or not present, will not be used by optimizer.

Allow pinning of keys so they will not be moved by the optimizer. Could use
a constraint, but that's inefficient. Better if the search code could just
skip pinned and unused keys.

If Shift is needed, insert it in the key sequence like a one-shot key. If
there is one Shift key per hand, use the one opposite the next letter.

If there is one Space key per hand, use the one opposite the next letter.

Enable an experiment with auto-shift after Space using same-hand Space.

P2: Add support for additional layers for symbols on small layouts. For each
layer (Shift, L3, L4) let user specify whether the layer is independent of
the base layer or tied to it. If tied, symbols move together with base-layer
movement. Otherwise they move separately.

Ideas for implementing 2.0
==========================

Need to stop converting text to lower-case when generating corpora.

Variable layou size requires a more flexible definition of a layout, maybe by
passing around slices instead of fixed- size array references.

For efficiency, layout could still be a maximally sized array with around
56 elements. Or a heap-allocated vector, if allocation is not on a
performance-critical path. The layout determines KeyProps, including position
and handedness of keys.

Make sure to eliminate any remaining use of modulo division to determine
rows, columns or fingers.

Parsing of layouts needs some new representations of Shift and Space that now
need to be made explicit. Use double-same-charaters for special functions of
keys because they should never occur in real layouts:

  -- Unused
  __ Space
  ^^ Shift
  ## Layer 3
  $$ Layer 4

Shift and layer keys can be combined with symbols for ModTap/LayerTap, e.g.
^^tT.

Also more layers means up to four symbols per key.

Add the layout type to the .kbl files.

Eval and rank commands ignore layouts of the wrong type. Add an option to
enable all layout types. --any

Pinned keys should be specified in a separate option because it's not
intrinsic to the layout. Unused, Space, Shift and Layer keys are implicitly
locked.

Pinned keys and independent layers need to be honored by EvalModel::neighbor.
Need lists of movable keys and movable finger_keys. Also need a better
implementation of layout.shuffle rather than just relying on the array or
slice method.

Need a new way of printing bigger layouts. Will need more rows for layouts
with thumb keys and potentially a number row. Full 60% layout is 14 keys wide
or 14x4=56 columns wide. That leaves only 24 columns for the stats table.
Needs to be reformatted. May need to go beyond 80 columns at least for wider
60% layouts. Ignore unused keys. How to represent ModTaps? Maybe by changing
the [  ] brackets around keys to a different character. Or using the free
space in single-letter keys.

Support for more layers will make layout-printing messy. Maybe leave out the
extra layers. Or add more width to keys, or make keys double-height.

